	affiliations = {Ltd, Hangzhou, China; Zhejiang Sci-Tech University, School of Civil Engineering and Architecture, Hangzhou, China},
	abstract = {Foundation pit engineering poses considerable hurdles owing to its intricate building conditions, fluctuating resource demands, and elevated safety hazards. Conventional scheduling techniques, such the Critical Path Method (CPM), are inflexible and do not accommodate real-time modifications, frequently leading to inefficiencies and delays. This work presents a dynamic scheduling system that merges Internet of Things (IoT) technologies with advanced Artificial Intelligence (AI) models to handle these difficulties. The IoT design integrates edge computing to preprocess real-time data from displacement, stress, and environmental sensors, therefore minimizing latency and enhancing data dependability. The system utilizes a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network for delay prediction, attaining an accuracy of 92%, alongside a hybrid optimization approach that integrates reinforcement learning and Critical Path Method (CPM) for dynamic resource allocation. Experimental validation on actual and simulated foundation pit projects indicates that the system increases resource utilization by 18%, decreases delay rates by 24%, and improves scheduling efficiency relative to conventional methods. The system demonstrates significant adaptability to unfavorable conditions, including equipment malfunctions and severe weather, so providing dependable and effective project management. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
