	affiliations = {Ramaiah Institute of Technology, Prof. and Dept of CSE., Bengaluru, India},
	abstract = {Environmental preservation and sustainable waste management depend on the effective separation of dry and wet household garbage. The majority of homes do not properly grasp how to separate waste into categories such as hazardous, recyclable, wet and dry. There are not many affordable Artificial Intelligence (AI) powered waste detection and sorting systems available to households. To overcome these types of household problems, in this study's deep learning-based classification system using Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), VGG16, and ResNet architectures are used to automatically identify and separate dry and wet garbage at the home level. To train and assess the performance of each model, a labeled dataset of garbage images are collected and preprocessed. To improve accuracy and cut down on training time, VGG16 and ResNet use transfer learning. According to the results, CNN provides lightweight deployment choices for environments with limited resources, whereas ResNet achieves excellent accuracy with 98.5% of Training accuracy and 96.3% testing accuracy. By integrating the suggested system with smart bins, waste can be automatically separated in real time, minimizing human error and encouraging recycling. This strategy promotes sustainability at the home level and fits in with smart city projects that employ AI to expedite garbage management. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
