	affiliations = {University of Petroleum and Energy Studies, Dehradun, India},
	abstract = {Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) is a critical global health threat driven by extensive antibiotic use. The chapter "Role of Artificial Intelligence in Fighting Antimicrobial Resistance" explores how AI can combat AMR. It traces AI's history in healthcare, from the Dendral project to IBM Watson, showcasing AI's evolving role in diagnosing and treating infections. The chapter delves into antimicrobial peptides (AMPs) and their dual mechanisms, highlighting their potential as alternatives to traditional antibiotics. It covers AI's applications in predicting pediatric infectious diseases, optimizing antibiotic administration, forecasting resistance, and aiding in protein structure prediction and drug design. Challenges such as health communication complexities, poverty's role, professional awareness gaps, and international data discrepancies are addressed, emphasizing the need for integrated strategies. Despite AI's potential, the chapter notes its limitations, including data quality issues and ethical considerations. In conclusion, AI's integration into healthcare can enhance AMR management by improving diagnosis, treatment, and drug development, offering a promising approach to controlling resistant infections. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
