	abstract = {This study looks at how reskilling programs influence people's knowledge of artificial intelligence (AI). Rapid advancements in specialized fields during this time have increased interest in contributions connected to artificial intelligence for various organizations. As a result, businesses are launching reskilling initiatives to provide their staff with essential computer-based intelligence expertise. This study aims to assess how successfully these initiatives improve people's knowledge and skills related to concepts, tools, and applications of artificial intelligence. Through qualitative and quantitative research methods, such as surveys, focus groups, and contextual inquiries, the evaluation evaluates how reskilling initiatives aid participants in achieving man-made cognitive competency. This focus also looks at program design, delivery methods, teacher experience, and hierarchical support as factors that affect reskilling project outcomes. The judgments made by the Exploration should be observed by policymakers, educators, managers, and anyone attempting to investigate the rapidly evolving landscape of artificial intelligence-driven work settings and breakthroughs. Understanding how reskilling exercises affect simulated intelligence commonalities will finally help businesses create and manage more effective training programs to equip their employees for the future of man-made intelligence. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
