	year = {2025},
	pages = {137 - 142},
	doi = {10.1109/IC3ECSBHI63591.2025.10991184},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105008421537&doi=10.1109%2FIC3ECSBHI63591.2025.10991184&partnerID=40&md5=c02c58cf5641906717b83ec129efd05a},
	affiliations = {JIET Jodhpur, Department Of Technology, Jodhpur, India; Rbmi, Department of Computer Application, Greater Noida, India; Guru Gobind Singh Indraprastha University, New Delhi, India; Mangalmay Group of Institutions, Greater Noida, India; Jagannath International Management School, Department of Information Technology, New Delhi, India; GL Bajaj Institute of Management, Greater Noida, India},
	abstract = {The rapid increase of global health burden from chronic diseases, like type 2 diabetes, hypertension, cancer, etc. Developing an individualized treatment plan for these diseases is fundamental to improving patient prognosis. Unfortunately, traditional treatment planning takes a long time, and it can be an expensive process that might not consider the particular patient requirements/preferences. This study aims to develop a novel method involving artificial intelligence (AI) and multi-objective optimization-based treatment planning using real-time data. The authors suggested a tool for combining clinical data, patient-specific information, and treatment guidelines to build personalized interventions aligned with medical efficacy and the preferred trade-offs from patients. This AI will work at processing the data for each patient on an ongoing basis and updating treatment plans in real time to ensure they stay dynamic and adaptive. In addition, the multi-objective optimization approach will enable better plan tailoring to patient-specific objectives regarding quality of life, medication compliance, and cost-effectiveness. This project's projected impacts are considerable and can transform chronic disease management by improving treatment effectiveness, reducing patient and selfmanagement burdens and burden on healthcare systems with overall improved patient outcomes. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
