	affiliations = {Instituto Superior Técnico, Lisbon, Portugal; University of the West of Scotland, Engineering & Physical Sciences, Ayr, United Kingdom; Macquarie University, Faculty of Science and Engineering, Sydney, Australia; Quchan University of Technology, Department of Industrial Engineering, Quchan, Iran; University of Wollongong, Wollongong, Australia; Rivers State University, Department of Marine Engineering, Port Harcourt, Nigeria; Memorial University of Newfoundland, Faculty of Engineering & Applied Science, St John's, Canada; Hanoi University of Mining and Geology, Faculty of Petroleum and Energy, Hanoi, Viet Nam; Health and Safety, Department of Environment, Vancouver, Canada; Toronto Metropolitan University, School of Occupational and Public Health, Toronto, Canada; Regional Maritime University, Ghana, Department of Marine Engineering, Accra, Ghana; University of Electronic Science and Technology of China, School of Automation Engineering, Chengdu, China; Universiti Teknologi Malaysia, Faculty of Mechanical Engineering, Johor Bahru, Malaysia; Polytechnique Montréal, Department of Mathematics and Industrial Engineering, Montreal, Canada; Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University, Prescott, Department of Safety Science, Prescott, United States; Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University, Prescott, Prescott, United States; School of Hotel and Tourism Management, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong, Hong Kong; Universität Wien, Department of Business Decisions and Analytics, Vienna, Austria; Western Norway University of Applied Sciences, Department of Fire Safety and HSE Engineering, Bergen, Norway; Aibel AS, Technical Safety, Haugesund, Norway; Thapar Institute of Engineering & Technology, School of Mathematics, Patiala, India},
	abstract = {Project risk management is pivotal in ensuring the success of endeavours within contemporary organizations. As businesses navigate an intricate and competitive landscape, integrating artificial intelligence (AI) introduces a paradigm shift in how project risks are recognized, evaluated, and addressed. This chapter go through into the multifaceted impact of AI on project risk management, reshaping strategies and enhancing decision-making processes to elevate project outcomes. By leveraging predictive analytics and machine learning, AI uncovers hidden patterns in historical project data, enabling proactive risk anticipation and simulation-based mitigation strategies. AI's cognitive capabilities expedite risk assessment by aggregating diverse data sources, fostering agility in response strategies through real-time communication and collaboration among project teams. Beyond operational facets, AI-driven algorithms continuously adapt to evolving risk landscapes, refining risk management strategies and resource allocation. Ethical considerations arise with the expanding role of AI, emphasizing the importance of transparency and accountability in algorithmic decision-making. This chapter envisions a dynamic convergence of AI and project risk management, fostering a future where innovation harmonizes with human understanding, propelling projects towards excellence, resilience, and growth. © 2024 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
