	affiliations = {Charles University, Institute of Formal and Applied Linguistics, Prague, Czech Republic; Filozofická Fakulta Univerzita Karlova, Institute of Phonetics, Prague, Czech Republic; Czech Technical University in Prague, Department of Circuit Theory, Prague, Czech Republic; Všeobecná Fakultní Nemocnice v Praze, Department of Neurology, Prague, Czech Republic; University Hospital Bern, Department of Neurology & ARTORG Center, Bern, Switzerland; Center for Evidence-Based Imaging, Brookline, United States; Harvard Medical School, Department of Radiology, Boston, United States; Harvard Medical School, Department of Biomedical Informatics, Boston, United States; Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine, Baltimore, United States; Brigham and Women's Hospital, Center for Neurological Imaging, Boston, United States},
	abstract = {Background: An early diagnosis together with an accurate disease progression monitoring of multiple sclerosis is an important component of successful disease management. Prior studies have established that multiple sclerosis is correlated with speech discrepancies. Early research using objective acoustic measurements has discovered measurable dysarthria. Method: The objective was to determine the potential clinical utility of machine learning and deep learning/AI approaches for the aiding of diagnosis, biomarker extraction and progression monitoring of multiple sclerosis using speech recordings. A corpus of 65 MS-positive and 66 healthy individuals reading the same text aloud was used for targeted acoustic feature extraction utilizing automatic phoneme segmentation. A series of binary classification models was trained, tuned, and evaluated regarding their Accuracy and area-under-the-curve. Results: The Random Forest model performed best, achieving an Accuracy of 0.82 on the validation dataset and an area-under-the-curve of 0.76 across 5 k-fold cycles on the training dataset. 5 out of 7 acoustic features were statistically significant. Conclusion: Machine learning and artificial intelligence in automatic analyses of voice recordings for aiding multiple sclerosis diagnosis and progression tracking seems promising. Further clinical validation of these methods and their mapping onto multiple sclerosis progression is needed, as well as a validating utility for English-speaking populations. © 2022 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Biomedical; Dysarthria; Machine learning; Multiple sclerosis; Phonetics; Speech acoustics; Technology assessment},
